  <h1>India’s Food Security — Buffer Stocks, PDS, and Nutrition Challenges</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Food security is central to India’s welfare and development agenda. In 2024, The Hindu examined India’s food security framework, which rests on buffer stocks, the Public Distribution System (PDS), and nutrition programs. For UPSC, food security reflects the intersection of economy, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Buffer Stocks (FCI, 2024): ~55 million tonnes of rice and wheat, above norms.<br/>•	PDS Coverage: ~81 crore beneficiaries under NFSA (2013).<br/>•	Free Foodgrain Scheme: Extended till 2028, covering 5 kg per person/month.<br/>•	Nutrition Gaps: 35% children stunted, 18% wasted (NFHS-5).<br/>•	Global Rank: 111/125 in Global Hunger Index (2023).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Leakages in PDS: Diversion, ghost beneficiaries, and corruption.<br/>2.	Nutritional Deficit: PDS dominated by cereals; poor protein and micronutrients.<br/>3.	Regional Imbalances: Surplus grain in some states, scarcity in others.<br/>4.	Storage Losses: FCI godowns often overstocked, wastage risks.<br/>5.	Fiscal Burden: Food subsidy bill ~₹2.3 lakh crore (2023–24).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	NFSA (2013): Legal right to subsidised food.<br/>•	PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana (PMGKAY): Free foodgrains since COVID.<br/>•	One Nation, One Ration Card (ONORC): Portability across states.<br/>•	Poshan Abhiyaan (2018): Nutrition-focused mission.<br/>•	Fortified Rice Program: Mandatory fortification in PDS and ICDS.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Expanded Coverage: ~2/3rd of population under NFSA.<br/>•	Portability Success: ONORC benefited 8 crore migrants in 2024.<br/>•	Digitalisation: Aadhaar and e-PoS reduced some leakages.<br/>•	Nutrition Focus: Fortified foods scaling up.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Quality Issues: Grains often poor in quality and storage.<br/>•	Exclusion Errors: Vulnerable groups left out of ration cards.<br/>•	Diet Diversity: Focus on calories, neglecting nutrition.<br/>•	State Variations: Kerala, Tamil Nadu effective; Bihar, UP weak.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Social Justice: Ensures right to food and dignity.<br/>•	Economic Stability: Protects poor against inflation shocks.<br/>•	Human Capital: Better nutrition improves education and productivity.<br/>•	Political Economy: Food schemes shape electoral politics.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare delivery, NFSA.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Agriculture): Food security, PDS.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Food security is the foundation of human development.”<br/>o	“India’s hunger is not due to shortage of food but poor access and distribution.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Welfare state and rights-based approach.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Diversify PDS: Include pulses, oil, and millets.<br/>2.	Strengthen Storage: Silos, cold chains to reduce wastage.<br/>3.	Improve Targeting: Dynamic databases to minimise exclusion.<br/>4.	Link with Nutrition: Integrate PDS with Poshan Abhiyaan, ICDS.<br/>5.	Fiscal Reforms: Rationalise subsidies, invest in agri diversification.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s food security system has ensured survival but not full nutrition security. The next phase must go beyond calories to address malnutrition and diet diversity. For UPSC aspirants, this theme illustrates how welfare, economy, and governance intersect in ensuring the right to food.</p>
